<div class="meta">Lesson 0014 · LLM engineering · from tools/bench_npc_models.py</div>
<h1>Benchmarking LLMs Against an Output Contract</h1>
<p>"Which local model should my NPCs use?" is answerable with numbers — if you
measure the thing your game actually depends on.</p>

<h2>The contract is the benchmark</h2>
<p>Generic leaderboards rank models on math, coding, and trivia. Your game
doesn't need any of that: it needs every reply to end with a parseable
<code>[[MOOD: happy]]</code> tag, stay in character for twenty turns, and
arrive fast on a laptop. That triple — <strong>format compliance, behavioral
consistency, latency</strong> — is the whole evaluation. Anything else is
someone else's benchmark.</p>
<div class="callout warn">First rule: benchmark the REAL contract. This project's brief
said "measure valid-JSON rate" — but the game doesn't use JSON mode; it parses
directive tags from plain text. Measuring JSON compliance would have ranked
models on a protocol the game never speaks. Always read the code before
writing the harness.</div>

<h2>One source of truth for the prompt</h2>
<p>A benchmark that hand-copies the system prompt drifts the moment someone
edits <code>renderSystemPrompt()</code>. This harness instead calls a 20-line
CLI (<code>persona_prompt</code>) that links the game's own core library and
prints exactly what an NPC would send. The harness can't drift because it has
no prompt of its own.</p>

<h2>The three metrics, concretely</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Metric</th><th>How it's measured</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>Compliance</td><td>N single-shot probes across all personas; a reply passes if it carries a known-keyword mood tag and no malformed directives (mirrors <code>parseDirectives</code> tolerance)</td><td>A non-compliant reply = an NPC with no facial expression and ignored actions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Consistency</td><td>A scripted 20-turn conversation scored for fourth-wall breaks ("as an AI..."), identity drift (wrong name), sustained tag compliance, and style-length conformance</td><td>Small models degrade over long context — turn 18 matters more than turn 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>Latency</td><td>Median time-to-first-token + total time (streamed)</td><td>TTFT is what the player feels; totals bound the conversation rhythm</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Proxy scoring and its honesty budget</h2>
<p>Fully-local means no GPT-4-as-judge. Regex proxies (fourth-wall phrases,
name checks, length bounds) are crude — they miss subtle character breaks and
false-positive on legitimate lines like "I'm no robot, honey." The discipline
is to <em>say so</em>: the report labels consistency as proxy-scored and keeps
the raw transcripts for spot-reading. A proxy you've documented is a metric; a
proxy you've forgotten is a lie.</p>

<h2>Design details that keep numbers trustworthy</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Same temperature as production</strong> (0.8) — benchmarking at 0
measures a model nobody ships.</li>
<li><strong>Deterministic scripts, not random prompts</strong> — reruns are
comparable; the 20-turn script deliberately includes mood shifts, action
requests, identity probes, and a "are you a robot?" trap.</li>
<li><strong>Failures score, they don't crash</strong> — a model that can't
load or times out gets skipped-with-reason, and the ranking proceeds.</li>
<li><strong>A weighted single score for ranking, raw numbers for judgment</strong>
— compliance dominates (it's binary player-facing breakage), consistency
second, latency as a tie-breaker with a cap so a fast-but-sloppy model can't
buy its way up.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout good">Takeaway: an LLM benchmark is just a test suite where the
assertions are statistical. Pin the prompt to the production code path, measure
the contract your parser enforces, state N, and let failure be data.</div>
